    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d31">

                <pb facs="0089" n="43" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg43"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/717a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President Wilson </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-08-13">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 13, 1917</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: Sir Cecil<anchor n="32"
                        xml:id="d31-f1"/> came to see me Saturday evening and handed me the enclosed
                    appeal by the Pope to the belligerents which he said would soon be
                        issued.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d31-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>My own impression is that this statement of peace terms emanates from
                    Austria-Hungary and is probably sanctioned by the German Government. It is
                    undoubtedly preliminary to the Stockholm Conference.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d31-f1" n="32" xml:id="d31-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Cecil Spring Rice, the British Ambassador.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d31-f2" n="33" xml:id="d31-f2-content">
                <p>For text of the Pope’s appeal, see <ref target="frus1917Supp02v01#pg_161">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1917, supp. 2, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 161</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
